WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.520
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:09.490
After reading a physics textbook on the laws
of fluid motion, with a little effort, we

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:15.670
can make a virtual world come alive by writing
a computer program that contains these laws,

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:20.199
resulting in beautiful fluid simulations like
the one you see here.

00:00:20.199 --> 00:00:24.830
The amount of detail we can simulate with
these programs is increasing every year, not

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:30.039
only due to the fact that hardware improves
over time, but also, the pace of progress

00:00:30.039 --> 00:00:33.240
in computer graphics research is truly remarkable.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:39.100
To simulate all these, many recent methods
build on top of a technique called the Material

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.610
Point Method.

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:45.670
This is a hybrid simulation technique that
uses both particles and grids to create these

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:51.870
beautiful animations, however, when used by
itself, we can come up with a bunch of phenomena

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.270
that it cannot simulate properly.

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:59.489
One such example is cracking and tearing phenomena,
which has been addressed in a previous paper

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:02.140
that we covered a few videos ago.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:08.579
With this, we can smash oreos, candy crabs,
pumpkins, and much, much more.

00:01:08.579 --> 00:01:13.179
In a few minutes, I will show you how to combine
some of these aspects of a simulation.

00:01:13.179 --> 00:01:18.729
It is going to be glorious…or, maybe, not
so much!

00:01:18.729 --> 00:01:20.920
Just give me a moment and you’ll see!

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:26.479
Beyond that, when using this material point
method, coupling problems frequently arise.

00:01:26.479 --> 00:01:31.399
This means that the sand is allowed to have
an effect on the fluid, but at the same time,

00:01:31.399 --> 00:01:36.259
as the fluid sloshes around, it also moves
the sand particles within.

00:01:36.259 --> 00:01:38.710
This is what we refer to as two-way coupling.

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:44.490
If it is implemented correctly, our simulated
honey will behave as real honey in the footage

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:47.639
here, and support the dipper.

00:01:47.639 --> 00:01:52.539
These are also not trivial to compute with
the Material Point Method and require specialized

00:01:52.539 --> 00:01:54.630
extensions to do so.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:57.070
So what else is there to do?

00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:02.729
This amazing new paper provides an extension
to handle simulating elastic objects, such

00:02:02.729 --> 00:02:08.721
as hair, rubber, and you will see that it
even works for skin simulations, and it can

00:02:08.721 --> 00:02:12.030
handle their interactions with other materials.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.400
So, why is this useful?

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.030
Well, we know that we can pull off simulating
a bunch of particles, and a jello simulation

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:24.660
separately, so it’s time for some experimentation!

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:29.830
This is the one I promised earlier, so let’s
try to put these two things together and see

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:31.540
what happens.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:39.680
It seems to start our okay, particles are
bouncing off of the jello…and then…uh-oh…look!

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:42.269
Many of them seem to get stuck.

00:02:42.269 --> 00:02:44.290
So can we fix this somehow?

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:48.290
Well, this, is where this new paper comes
into play.

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:49.290
Look here!

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:54.349
It starts out somewhat similarly, most of
the particles get pushed away from the jello,

00:02:54.349 --> 00:02:56.250
and then…look!

00:02:56.250 --> 00:03:03.069
Some of them indeed keep bouncing for a long-long
time, and, none of them are stuck to the jello.

00:03:03.069 --> 00:03:04.379
Glorious!

00:03:04.379 --> 00:03:09.569
We can see the same phenomenon here with three
jello blocks of different stiffness values.

00:03:09.569 --> 00:03:14.630
With this, we can also simulate more than
10 thousand bouncy hair strands, and to the

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:20.349
delight of a computer graphics researcher,
we can even throw snow into it and expect

00:03:20.349 --> 00:03:24.989
it to behave correctly.

00:03:24.989 --> 00:03:30.180
Braids work well too.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:33.969
And if you remember, I also promised some
skin simulation.

00:03:33.969 --> 00:03:39.239
And this demonstration is not only super fun,
for instance, the ones around this area are

00:03:39.239 --> 00:03:45.410
perhaps the most entertaining, but the information
density of this screen is just absolutely

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:46.689
amazing.

00:03:46.689 --> 00:03:51.950
As we go from bottom to top, you can see the
effect of the stiffness parameters, or in

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:57.849
other words, the higher we are, the stiffer
things become, and as we go from left to right,

00:03:57.849 --> 00:04:00.780
the effect of damping increases.

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:05.840
And you can see not only a bunch of combinations
of these two parameters, but you can also

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:11.249
compare many configurations against each other
at a glance, on the same screen.

00:04:11.249 --> 00:04:12.730
Loving it.

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:15.879
So how long does it take to simulate all this?

00:04:15.879 --> 00:04:21.070
Well, given that we are talking about an off-line
simulation technique, this is not designed

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:26.530
to run in real-time games, as the execution
time is typically not measured in frames per

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:32.080
second, but seconds per frame, and sometimes
even minutes per frame.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:37.080
However, having run simulations that contained
much fewer interactions than this that took

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:41.960
me several days to compute, I would argue
that these numbers are quite appealing for

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:43.960
a method of this class.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:49.070
Also, note that this is one of those papers
that makes the impossible possible for us,

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:53.500
and of course, as we always say around here,
two more papers down the line, and it will

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:55.630
be significantly improved.

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:58.060
For now, I am very impressed.

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:01.080
Time to fire up some elaborate jello simulations!

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:02.990
What a time to be alive!

